# EngramTier llama.cpp n-gram access-heat probe
#
# Wraps the speculative-decoding n-gram cache lookups with a per-call emitter
# that, when ENGRAM_TIER_PROFILE=1 is set in the environment, appends every
# looked-up n-gram key (one per line, colon-separated token ids) to the heat
# log at $ENGRAM_TIER_HEAT_LOG (default: engram-tier.heat.log). When the env
# var is unset the probe compiles to a branch-never-taken, so the same binary
# serves both profile and serve modes with zero overhead in serve.
#
# Keys with an n-gram size outside [2, 3] tokens are not emitted; the
# EngramTier heat model (internal/heat) covers the 2-3-token keyed table
# range and rejects other arities on load.
#
# Target: llama.cpp master 96ffdc41ceb055e1c2d3d96667ae6d9f0ccb710b
# (verified 2026-09-14) — the n-gram cache lives in common/ngram-cache.cpp
# (function common_ngram_cache_draft_partial and its try_draft helpers).
# A byte-exact copy of the upstream file at that revision is vendored at
# internal/probe/testdata/ngram-cache.cpp and the apply test checks this
# patch against it, so upstream drift is caught by `go test`.
#
# Apply from the llama.cpp repo root:  git apply --whitespace=fix <this-file>
# Revert with:                          git apply -R <this-file>
#
# The probe deliberately emits raw lookups (one key per line) and leaves
# aggregation to the Go heat collector — keeping "which keys are hot" a single
# source of truth in pure-Go, testable code.
